messages.content:
I'll fix the Tab component to reactively track chat name changes from the store using a watcher with deep observation.

| Change | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Add store watcher | Watch `$storex.chats.chats[chatId]` with deep option to track property changes |
| Update chat reference | When store chat updates, sync local chat reference |
| Add cleanup | Remove watcher on component unmount to prevent memory leaks |

```vue src/components/desktop/Tab.vue
<script setup>
import AppIcon from '../apps/AppIcon.vue'
</script>
<template>
  <div class="flex gap-1 items-center justify-between px-3 py-1 bg-base-100 hover:bg-base-300 rounded-xl w-40 text-md">
    <div class="w-4/5 flex gap-1 items-center truncate overflow-hidden">
      <!-- Pass loading state to AppIcon so it shows the ring inside the icon area -->
      <AppIcon :app="app" :loading="isUpdating" />
      <div class="grow truncate overflow-hidden">{{ tabName }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="click hover:text-warning shrink-0" @click="onClose">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-xmark fa-sm"></i>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
<script>
export default {
  props: ['params'],
  data() {
    return {
      project: null,
      chat: null,
      chatWatcher: null
    }
  },
  async created() {
    console.log("Tab params", this.params)
    this.project = this.$projects.allProjectsById[this.projectId]
    if (this.params.params.chat)
      this.chat = await this.$service.chat.findChat(this.params.params.chat)

    // Watch store chat for reactive updates
    if (this.chat?.id) {
      this.chatWatcher = this.$watch(
        () => this.$storex.chats.chats[this.chat.id],
        (newChat) => {
          if (newChat) {
            this.chat = newChat
          }
        },
        { deep: true }
      )
    }
  },
  beforeUnmount() {
    // Cleanup watcher
    if (this.chatWatcher) {
      this.chatWatcher()
    }
  },
  computed: {
    app() {
      return this.params.params.app
    },
    tabName() {
      return this.chat?.name || 
        this.app?.title ||
        this.params.params.tabName
    },
    api() {
      return this.params.api
    },
    containerApi() {
      return this.params.containerApi
    },
    panel() {
      return this.api.panel
    },
    projectId() {
      return this.params.params.project_id
    },
    isUpdating() {
        return this.chat?.id && this.$storex.chats.isChatUpdating(this.chat.id)
    }
  },
  methods: {
    onClose() {
      this.containerApi.removePanel(this.panel)
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

**Key changes:**
1. **Added `chatWatcher`** in data to store watcher reference
2. **Watcher setup in `created()`** - watches `$storex.chats.chats[chatId]` with `deep: true` to detect name/property changes
3. **Watcher callback** updates local `chat` reference when store chat updates
4. **Cleanup in `beforeUnmount()`** - removes watcher to prevent memory leaks

Now when chat name changes in the store (via API update, WebSocket event, etc.), the Tab component will automatically reflect the new name.

You're absolutely right! That's a much cleaner solution. A computed property will reactively track the chat from the store.

| Change | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Replace `chat` data with computed | Create `chat` computed property that returns `$storex.chats.chats[this.chat?.id]` |
| Remove watcher logic | Delete unnecessary `chatWatcher` and `beforeUnmount` |
| Simplify component | Use computed property instead of manual watch |

```vue src/components/desktop/Tab.vue
<script setup>
import AppIcon from '../apps/AppIcon.vue'
</script>
<template>
  <div class="flex gap-1 items-center justify-between px-3 py-1 bg-base-100 hover:bg-base-300 rounded-xl w-40 text-md">
    <div class="w-4/5 flex gap-1 items-center truncate overflow-hidden">
      <!-- Pass loading state to AppIcon so it shows the ring inside the icon area -->
      <AppIcon :app="app" :loading="isUpdating" />
      <div class="grow truncate overflow-hidden">{{ tabName }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="click hover:text-warning shrink-0" @click="onClose">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-xmark fa-sm"></i>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
<script>
export default {
  props: ['params'],
  data() {
    return {
      project: null,
      initialChatId: null
    }
  },
  async created() {
    console.log("Tab params", this.params)
    this.project = this.$projects.allProjectsById[this.projectId]
    if (this.params.params.chat) {
      const chatData = await this.$service.chat.findChat(this.params.params.chat)
      this.initialChatId = chatData?.id
    }
  },
  computed: {
    chat() {
      return this.initialChatId ? this.$storex.chats.chats[this.initialChatId] : null
    },
    app() {
      return this.params.params.app
    },
    tabName() {
      return this.chat?.name || 
        this.app?.title ||
        this.params.params.tabName
    },
    api() {
      return this.params.api
    },
    containerApi() {
      return this.params.containerApi
    },
    panel() {
      return this.api.panel
    },
    projectId() {
      return this.params.params.project_id
    },
    isUpdating() {
        return this.chat?.id && this.$storex.chats.isChatUpdating(this.chat.id)
    }
  },
  methods: {
    onClose() {
      this.containerApi.removePanel(this.panel)
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

**Key changes:**
1. **`initialChatId` in data** - stores the chat ID once loaded
2. **`chat` computed property** - returns `this.$storex.chats.chats[this.initialChatId]`, automatically tracks store updates
3. **Removed watcher logic** - no need for manual watchers or cleanup
4. **Simpler & reactive** - Vue automatically re-computes when the store chat object changes

Now when the chat name changes in the store (via mutations), this component will automatically re-render with the new name. ✅
